{"pagination":{"maxPageableSet":"10000","numFound":2,"query":"name=Nuland&limit=10&title=How We Die","limit":10,"start":0},"items":{"mods":[{"@version":"3.8","titleInfo":[{"title":"How we die ","subTitle":"reflections on life's final chapter "},{"@type":"alternative","title":"Reflections on life's final chapter"}],"name":{"@usage":"primary","@type":"personal","namePart":"Nuland, Sherwin B.","role":{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"creator"}}},"typeOfResource":"text","genre":[{"@authority":"Medical Subject Headings","#text":"Autobiography"},{"@authority":"fast","#text":"Autobiographies"},{"@authority":"lcgft","#text":"Autobiographies"},{"@authority":"rvmgf","#text":"Autobiographies."}],"originInfo":{"place":[{"placeTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"nyu"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"New York (State)"}]},{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"New York "}}],"issuance":"monographic","agent":[{"namePart":"A.A. Knopf "},{"namePart":"Distributed by Random House, Inc."}],"dateIssued":"1994","edition":"1st ed."},"language":{"languageTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"English"}]},"physicalDescription":[{"form":{"@authority":"marcform","#text":"print"},"extent":"xviii, 278 p. : ill. ; 25 cm."},{"form":{"@type":"Illustrative content","#text":"illustration"}}],"abstract":{"@type":"Review","#text":"\"There is a vast literature on death and dying, but there are few reliable accounts of the ways in which we die. The intimate account of how various diseases take away life, offered in How We Die, is not meant to prompt horror or terror but to demythologize the process of dying to help us rid ourselves of that fear of the terra incognita.\" \"Though the avenues of death - AIDS, cancer, heart attack, Alzheimer's, accident, and stroke - are common, each of us will die in a way different from any that has gone before. Each one of death's diverse appearances is as distinctive as that singular face we each show during our lives. Behind each death is a story.\" \"In How We Die, Sherwin B. Nuland, a surgeon and teacher of medicine, tells some stories of dying that reveal not only why someone dies but how. He offers a portrait of the experience of dying that makes clear the choices that can be made to allow each of us his or her own death.\"--Jacket."},"tableOfContents":{"@type":"Contents","#text":"The strangled heart -- A valentine--and how it fails -- Three score and ten -- Doors to death of the aged -- Alzheimer's disease -- Murder and serenity -- Accidents, suicide, and euthanasia -- A story of AIDS -- The life of a virus and the death of a man -- The malevolence of cancer -- Hope and the cancer patient -- The lessons learned."},"note":[{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"Sherwin B. Nuland."},"Includes index."],"subject":[{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Death"},{"@authority":"mesh","topic":"Attitude to Death"},{"@authority":"mesh","topic":"Death"}],"classification":[{"@authority":"lcc","#text":"BD444 .N85 1994"},{"@authority":"nlm","#text":"1994 A-386"},{"@authority":"nlm","#text":"WT 104 N969h 1994"},{"@edition":"20","@authority":"ddc","#text":"616.07/8"}],"identifier":[{"@type":"isbn","#text":"0679414614 :"},{"@type":"lccn","#text":"^^^93024590^"},{"@type":"oclc","#text":"28148780"}],"relatedItem":{"@otherType":"HOLLIS record","location":{"url":"https://id.lib.harvard.edu/alma/990038858020203941/catalog"}},"location":[{"physicalLocation":{"@displayLabel":"Harvard repository","@type":"repository","@valueURI":"http://isni.org/isni/000000046073724X","#text":"Cabot Science Library, Harvard University"},"shelfLocator":"BD444 .N85 1994"},{"physicalLocation":{"@displayLabel":"Harvard repository","@type":"repository","@valueURI":"http://isni.org/isni/0000000121705139","#text":"Harvard Divinity School Library, Harvard University"},"shelfLocator":" BD444   .N85 1994 "},{"physicalLocation":{"@displayLabel":"Harvard reposit